1
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:05,810
Hello and welcome to this new session about Emvco.

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,970
Objectives and concepts.

3
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:18,800
The EMV standard established by Emvco defines the interaction between payment cards and terminals for

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:26,240
secure transactions to enable universal acceptance of chip cards for credit and debit.

5
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:34,160
EMV defines minimum functionality in order to get interoperability between card and terminal.

6
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:39,050
Each entity involved has specific objectives.

7
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:50,090
So that's why the first part about this presentation we will talk about the EMV objectives.

8
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:56,750
So there are several actors involved in EMV communication.

9
00:00:57,020 --> 00:00:59,720
These actors are.

10
00:00:59,730 --> 00:01:00,210
Issuer.

11
00:01:00,210 --> 00:01:00,930
Bank.

12
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:02,010
Acquirer.

13
00:01:02,010 --> 00:01:02,790
Bank.

14
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:06,990
Personal personalization or card customization.

15
00:01:07,590 --> 00:01:12,750
Card reader or terminal and card holders or users.

16
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,470
So first of all we will talk about issuer bank objectives.

17
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:23,580
So the first point is card security.

18
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:33,240
In fact it ensures that issued cards are secure, preventing cloning, fraud and unauthorized use.

19
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:36,390
Second, we have personalization.

20
00:01:36,570 --> 00:01:45,030
It configure the card with the necessary data, for example cryptographic keys and application data.

21
00:01:45,930 --> 00:01:50,340
We do this configuration to make it functional for EMV transaction.

22
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:54,450
Third point is transaction authorization.

23
00:01:54,780 --> 00:02:03,040
It maintains control over whether transactions are approved or declined based on risk management and

24
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:13,960
cardholder authentication, and last data integrity, which ensures data sent from the car to the bank

25
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:18,400
remains intact and unaltered during transmission.

26
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:30,130
The second point about objectives we have is acquire bank objectives.

27
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:34,420
So first of all we have transaction acceptance.

28
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:42,370
It enables merchants to process transactions transactions securely by managing the connection between

29
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:45,130
the payment terminal and the issuer bank.

30
00:02:46,030 --> 00:02:55,180
Second is compliance, which ensures that all terminals and infrastructure are compliant with EMV standards

31
00:02:55,180 --> 00:03:01,720
for secure data transmission and encryption, then we have fraud detection.

32
00:03:02,890 --> 00:03:10,240
It collaborates with the issuer bank to implement mechanisms for identifying and preventing fraudulent

33
00:03:10,270 --> 00:03:11,260
transactions.

34
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:21,040
And finally settlement facilitate the proper settlement of funds from the issuer bank to the merchant's

35
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:21,850
account.

36
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:29,500
The third objective is the personalization objectives.

37
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:38,200
So cardholder data programming personalize each card with a unique account number, cryptographic keys,

38
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:40,300
and issuer specific applications.

39
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:49,450
Then we have security setup implement encryption and authentication methods such as Pin or biometric

40
00:03:49,450 --> 00:03:53,140
verification to secure cardholder identity.

41
00:03:53,950 --> 00:04:02,540
Third, we have application loading, which install necessary EMV applications for processing credit,

42
00:04:02,570 --> 00:04:05,180
debit or other forms of payment.

43
00:04:06,350 --> 00:04:14,510
And finally compatibility, which ensures that the card can communicate with EMV compliant readers across

44
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:16,490
different regions and networks.

45
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:21,230
Then we have card objectives.

46
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:30,140
So first of all, regarding card objectives objectives, we have secure payment which store cryptographic

47
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:35,870
information that authenticates the card during transactions, ensuring secure communication with the

48
00:04:35,870 --> 00:04:36,470
terminal.

49
00:04:37,250 --> 00:04:46,160
Second, cardholder verification which support various verification methods, Pin signature, etc. to

50
00:04:46,190 --> 00:04:47,480
authenticate the user.

51
00:04:49,190 --> 00:04:57,720
Third point is the interoperability IT function correctly across a wide range of terminals globally

52
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:00,120
by adhering to the EMV standards.

53
00:05:00,750 --> 00:05:07,830
And finally, transaction integrity, which ensures transaction data is not modified or intercepted

54
00:05:07,830 --> 00:05:10,110
during the payment process.

55
00:05:16,530 --> 00:05:20,220
The first point here is the reader objectives.

56
00:05:20,730 --> 00:05:23,790
So first we have transaction authorization.

57
00:05:24,090 --> 00:05:31,020
It facilitates communication between the card and the acquirer bank ensuring data is correctly transmitted

58
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:32,190
for authorization.

59
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:43,050
Then we have secure environment provides a secure environment for data exchange to prevent skimming

60
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:47,040
or unauthorized access to sensitive cardholder data.

61
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:53,190
Third is compliance with EMV specifications.

62
00:05:53,550 --> 00:06:01,270
It ensures the terminal meets EMV standards, allowing it to accept chip based cards securely.

63
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:09,550
Finally, you have cardholder verification, which supports Pin entry and other verification methods

64
00:06:09,550 --> 00:06:12,790
to authenticate the cardholder during the transaction.

65
00:06:15,610 --> 00:06:21,940
The last objective here is the user objectives or cardholder objectives.

66
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:29,680
So first we have security trust that their card provides enhanced protection against fraud, such as

67
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:34,360
preventing unauthorized transactions through secure chip technology.

68
00:06:34,570 --> 00:06:42,580
Second is convenience easily complete transactions using contact or contactless EMV enabled cards with

69
00:06:42,580 --> 00:06:46,360
minimal friction, for example pin entry or tap to pay.

70
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:55,690
The third key here is the interoperability, so use the card across multiple countries and terminals

71
00:06:55,690 --> 00:07:03,880
that comply with the EMV standard, ensuring board usability and finally authentication control, which

72
00:07:03,880 --> 00:07:07,900
we verify their identity securely during transactions.

73
00:07:07,930 --> 00:07:16,480
Protecting against misuse if the card is lost or stolen, for example through pins or biometrics.

74
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:26,050
So each entity here plays a key role in making the EMV ecosystem secure and efficient, working together

75
00:07:26,050 --> 00:07:30,610
to ensure the integrity, authenticity, and convenience of payment transactions.

76
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:41,890
So what about EMV concepts?

77
00:07:42,250 --> 00:07:50,830
EMV concepts refer to various components and ideas that are essential to understanding how the EMV standard

78
00:07:50,830 --> 00:07:53,180
works Artworks within the payment ecosystem.

79
00:07:54,200 --> 00:08:02,300
These concepts cover the technical, functional and security aspects of EMV, making it a robust framework

80
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:06,560
for secure and interoperable card based transactions.

81
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:09,860
So here are the key EMV concepts.

82
00:08:10,010 --> 00:08:19,700
First, we have Integrated Circuit Card ICC, commonly referred to as a chip card.

83
00:08:19,730 --> 00:08:27,410
The ICC is a smart card embedded with a microchip that stores data securely and performs cryptographic

84
00:08:27,410 --> 00:08:29,510
functions, as you have seen before.

85
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:38,270
Its function is that, unlike magnetic stripe cards, ICS generate dynamic data for each transaction,

86
00:08:38,299 --> 00:08:41,419
making them more secure and harder to clone.

87
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:47,600
Second, we have contact and contactless transactions.

88
00:08:48,830 --> 00:08:55,710
So contact involves inserting the chip card into a terminal for the entire duration of the transaction.

89
00:08:56,910 --> 00:09:00,600
The card and terminal communicate through physical contact.

90
00:09:01,830 --> 00:09:09,600
However, contactless involves tapping or waving the card near a terminal equipped with near-field communication

91
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:10,350
technology.

92
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:19,050
NFC technology it allows for quicker transactions as the card does not need to be physically inserted.

93
00:09:20,040 --> 00:09:24,510
Then we have cardholder verification method CVM.

94
00:09:25,830 --> 00:09:33,210
So this method are the methods used to verify the identity of the cardholder during a transaction.

95
00:09:34,830 --> 00:09:45,300
The types are Pin personal identification number signature mostly phased out in some regions, and biometrics

96
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:49,390
for example fingerprint face recognition for mobile payments.

97
00:09:49,870 --> 00:09:55,720
The purpose is that it ensures that the person using the card is the legitimate card holder.

98
00:09:57,490 --> 00:10:00,940
The fourth point here is cryptography.

99
00:10:10,090 --> 00:10:15,910
So cryptography is the process of using algorithms to encode and decode that.

100
00:10:16,630 --> 00:10:22,960
Its role in EMV is that EMV uses cryptographic algorithm to secure transactions.

101
00:10:23,290 --> 00:10:30,670
Each transaction creates dynamic data, ensuring that the information cannot be reused by fraudsters.

102
00:10:32,950 --> 00:10:40,780
The key management is that EMV relies on symmetric and asymmetric keys to authenticate transactions,

103
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:45,820
secure holder secure cardholder data, and protect against fraud.

104
00:10:53,510 --> 00:10:57,110
After that we have offline data authentication.

105
00:10:58,370 --> 00:11:05,000
So authentication performed by the terminal without contacting the issuer's host system is the offline

106
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:06,380
data authentication.

107
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:10,100
The types of this.

108
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:13,040
We have static data authentication.

109
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:18,200
The card contains a preloaded cryptographic signature used for verification.

110
00:11:18,950 --> 00:11:28,160
Then we have dynamic data authentication more secure than SDA as it uses dynamically generated cryptographic

111
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:30,680
values for each transaction.

112
00:11:31,970 --> 00:11:40,160
The purpose of the offline data authentication is that it allows for card authentication in environments

113
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:43,490
where online connectivity is not always available.

114
00:11:45,350 --> 00:11:48,860
The sixth point here is transaction flow.

115
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:57,110
So when a card is inserted or tapped, the terminal selects the appropriate payment application.

116
00:11:57,140 --> 00:11:59,750
For example credit or debit from the card.

117
00:12:02,150 --> 00:12:09,080
The initiation here is that the terminal initiates the transaction requesting necessary data from the

118
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:09,530
card.

119
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:23,060
The seventh step here is the personalization.

120
00:12:23,990 --> 00:12:31,280
The personalization is the process of loading an EMV card with a specific data and cryptographic keys

121
00:12:31,310 --> 00:12:32,900
needed for transaction.

122
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:40,550
Its role is that personalization makes each card unique to its holder by embedding account details,

123
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:43,340
security keys, and issuer specific data.

124
00:12:45,110 --> 00:12:47,010
Then we have authorization.

125
00:12:47,970 --> 00:12:51,210
We have two types online and offline authorization.

126
00:12:51,510 --> 00:12:58,320
Regarding online authorization, the terminal communicates with the issuing banks server to authenticate

127
00:12:58,320 --> 00:12:59,220
the transaction.

128
00:12:59,970 --> 00:13:05,760
The card generates a cryptographic value which is verified by the issuer.

129
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:13,200
Regarding the offline authorization, transactions are authorized by the terminal without contacting

130
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:14,610
the issuer server.

131
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:18,000
Often used in locations with limited connectivity.

132
00:13:19,410 --> 00:13:24,330
The card generates an application cryptogram for offline validation.

133
00:13:25,140 --> 00:13:27,120
Then we have tokenization.

134
00:13:27,570 --> 00:13:34,770
So tokenization is a process where sensitive card details like the primary account number are replaced

135
00:13:34,770 --> 00:13:37,710
with a unique temporary token for a transaction.

136
00:13:38,610 --> 00:13:44,280
Its role is that it enhances security, especially for mobile and online payments.

137
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:48,940
By ensuring that the actual card details are not exposed during the transaction.

138
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:53,860
The 10th point here is the EMV kernel.

139
00:13:54,910 --> 00:14:01,210
So the NV kernel is the software within a payment terminal that processes EMV transactions.

140
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:08,320
Its function is that the kernel facilitates communication between the card and the term, ensuring that

141
00:14:08,350 --> 00:14:13,600
EMV transactions comply with the security and data exchange protocols.

142
00:14:15,370 --> 00:14:19,450
After that, we have application identifier a ID.

143
00:14:24,040 --> 00:14:31,540
So the ID is a unique string of data that identifies a specific payment application on an NV card.

144
00:14:31,570 --> 00:14:39,160
Its role is that the terminal uses the ID to select the correct application on the card, allowing for

145
00:14:39,160 --> 00:14:41,620
multiple applications on one card.

146
00:14:50,120 --> 00:14:52,400
Then we have risk management.

147
00:14:52,940 --> 00:15:00,830
So with the risk management is the processes carried out by the terminal to determine whether to authorize

148
00:15:00,830 --> 00:15:04,850
decline or send a transaction online for further processing.

149
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:10,880
The elements of this process are three.

150
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:13,070
So first of all we have floor limit.

151
00:15:14,930 --> 00:15:19,130
Floor limit is the maximum transaction value that can be approved offline.

152
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:27,500
Then we have the velocity check limits the number of transactions that can occur within a certain time

153
00:15:27,530 --> 00:15:28,100
frame.

154
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:31,250
The third point is the exception file.

155
00:15:32,390 --> 00:15:39,500
Terminals may check if the card is on an exception list, such as being reported, lost, or stolen.

156
00:15:40,850 --> 00:15:44,000
Then we have the EMV tokenization framework.

157
00:15:44,690 --> 00:15:53,060
So the EMV tokenization framework is a system introduced by Emvco to standardize how tokenized payments

158
00:15:53,780 --> 00:15:54,890
are handled.

159
00:15:54,920 --> 00:16:03,530
Its purpose is that it protects sensitive card data by ensuring that tokens can only be used within

160
00:16:03,530 --> 00:16:05,000
specific parameters.

161
00:16:08,210 --> 00:16:13,220
Last but not least, we have the Secure Remote Commerce S.r.c.

162
00:16:16,070 --> 00:16:23,240
It's an EMV standard that streamlines e-commerce payments by offering a universal checkout process that

163
00:16:23,240 --> 00:16:26,600
secures stores card details.

164
00:16:28,610 --> 00:16:31,520
It's often referred to as click to pay.

165
00:16:31,550 --> 00:16:37,610
It provides an easy, secure way to complete transactions without re-entering card details for each

166
00:16:37,610 --> 00:16:38,420
per case.

167
00:16:39,560 --> 00:16:40,790
Finally.

168
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:42,510
Card authentication.

169
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:51,810
So card authentication is the process of verifying that the card presented is legitimate.

170
00:16:52,470 --> 00:16:53,670
We have two types.

171
00:16:53,670 --> 00:16:56,100
The first one is static authentication.

172
00:16:57,900 --> 00:17:01,260
It's using preconfigured data for validation.

173
00:17:01,800 --> 00:17:08,730
Also we have dynamic authentication which is using dynamic transaction specific data for better security.

174
00:17:10,710 --> 00:17:21,089
All these concepts together form the core framework of how the EMV system operates to deliver secure,

175
00:17:21,089 --> 00:17:25,200
interoperable payment experiences globally.

